\section{Basic, Stochastic, and Mini-Batch Gradient Descent}
\label{sec:gd}

Implement and compare gradient descent methods for logistic regression. See \citet{ruder2016overview} for pseudo-code and details on the algorithms.

Training a neural network follows the same pattern: define a loss, compute its gradients with respect to the weights and biases, and update them using an optimiser such as mini-batch gradient descent or Adam. Logistic regression provides a small model in which you can inspect these steps directly. In later neural-network assignments, libraries use backpropagation to compute the gradients automatically; the learning-rate, batch-size, and regularisation choices explored here remain important.

We are going to work with this data as a test case:

<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("binary")

binary$gre_sd <- (binary$gre - mean(binary$gre))/sd(binary$gre)
binary$gpa_sd <- (binary$gpa - mean(binary$gpa))/sd(binary$gpa)
X <- model.matrix(admit ~ gre_sd + gpa_sd, binary)
y <- binary$admit
@


\subsection{Implement the gradient for logistic regression}


The likelihood function for logistic regression is
\[
L(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) = \prod^n_{i=1} p_i^{y_i} (1 - p_i)^{1-y_i} \,,
\]
where
\[
\log \frac{p_i}{1-p_i} = \mathbf{x}_i \theta  \,,
\]
and $\mathbf{x}_i$ is the $i$th row of the design matrix $\mathbf{X}$ and $\theta \in \mathbb{R}^P$ is a $P \times 1$ column vector of parameters.

Maximizing the likelihood is equivalent to minimizing the \emph{mean negative log-likelihood}, which we denote by $\text{NLL}$ throughout this assignment:
\begin{align}
\text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X})
 &= -\frac{1}{n}\log L(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) \notag\\
 &= -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left[y_i\log(p_i)+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)\right] \notag\\
 &= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \left[\log(1+\exp(\mathbf{x}_i\theta))-y_i\mathbf{x}_i\theta\right].
\end{align}
This is the binary cross-entropy averaged over observations. Both the loss and its gradient use this mean convention; no additional division by $n$ is needed after computing either one.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\thesubsection\alph*.}, ref=\thesubsection\alph*, leftmargin=*]
\item (4p) Derive the gradient of the mean negative log-likelihood $\text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X})$ with respect to $\theta$.\\\emph{Hint!} See \citet[Ch. 4.4]{hastie2009elements}.
\item (2p) Implement \texttt{nll\_grad(y, X, theta)}, returning the mean-loss gradient as a numeric vector in the same order as the columns of \texttt{X}. Include the function code and your actual outputs for the two calls below; compare them with the supplied results to four decimal places.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad <- function(y, X, theta){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0))
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad <- function(y, X, theta){
c("(Intercept)" = -0.0217, "gre_sd" = 0.0395, "gpa_sd" = 0.0426)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll_grad(y, X, theta = c(-1,0.5,0.5))
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "nll_grad")|

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Implement Gradient Descent}
Use the following function to evaluate the mean negative log-likelihood. It converts the total log-likelihood supplied by \texttt{uuml} to the mean loss defined above. Use \texttt{nll()} for loss evaluation throughout this assignment.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nll <- function(y, X, theta){
  -uuml::ll(y, X, theta) / length(y)
}
theta <- c(0, 0, 0)
nll(y, X, theta)
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "nll")|

\emph{Hint!} Gradient descent subtracts the gradient of the loss. Since \texttt{nll\_grad()} already returns the gradient of the mean negative log-likelihood, do not negate it or divide it by the number of observations again.

For SGD and mini-batch updates, evaluate \texttt{nll\_grad()} on the current observation or mini-batch, averaging over the observations supplied to that call. To monitor training, evaluate \texttt{nll()} on \emph{all training observations} at the end of each epoch, so every algorithm's loss history uses the same scale.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\thesubsection\alph*.}, ref=\thesubsection\alph*, leftmargin=*]
\item (1p) Fit logistic regression using \texttt{glm()} with \texttt{family = "binomial"}, and save the model as \texttt{glm\_fit}. Print the three maximum-likelihood parameter estimates.

\emph{Hint!} The design matrix \texttt{X} already includes an intercept so you need to remove that either from \texttt{X} or from the R formula using \texttt{y ~ -1 + X}.
\item (6p) Implement \emph{one} R function called \texttt{gradient\_descent()}. Use the arguments shown in the scaffold below and the gradient of the mean NLL \citep[see][for algorithm descriptions]{ruder2016overview}. The batch size determines the method:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{batch\_size = nrow(X)} gives ordinary full/batch gradient descent;
\item \texttt{batch\_size = 1} gives stochastic gradient descent;
\item \texttt{batch\_size = 10} gives mini-batch gradient descent.
\end{itemize}
Print the function once in your report. Reuse the figures and explanations from Experiments A and B as evidence, referring to them where relevant; no extra runs or output tables are required. Run for the specified number of epochs and assess convergence from the trajectories.

Each epoch must process every observation exactly once. For batches smaller than $n$, shuffle the observation indices once per epoch and split them into non-overlapping batches. Include any smaller final batch when $n$ is not divisible by the batch size. Make one parameter update per batch, using the mean gradient of that batch, and record the full-training-data mean NLL and parameters after the complete epoch.

\examplebox{One epoch and gradient dimensions}{
Five observations, three coefficients (intercept and two slopes), batch size 2, and shuffled order $4,1,5,2,3$ give:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
Update & Rows & Shape of batch $X$ & Gradient length \\
\hline
1 & $4,1$ & $2\times3$ & 3 \\
2 & $5,2$ & $2\times3$ & 3 \\
3 & $3$ & $1\times3$ & 3
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
One epoch makes three updates. Average each gradient over its actual batch size; record the full-data mean NLL after update 3.
}

\emph{Hint!} Use \texttt{X[batch, , drop = FALSE]} when selecting rows so a batch of size one remains a matrix. Start from $\theta=0$ for every call. Complete the batching and update steps in this scaffold:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
gradient_descent <- function(y, X, batch_size, eta, epochs){
  results <- matrix(0.0, ncol = ncol(X) + 2L, nrow = epochs)
  colnames(results) <- c("epoch", "nll", colnames(X))
  theta <- rep(0.0, ncol(X))
  for(epoch in seq_len(epochs)){

    # Choose the observation order and split it into batches.
    # For each batch, use nll_grad() to update theta.

    results[epoch, "epoch"] <- epoch
    results[epoch, "nll"] <- nll(y, X, theta)
    results[epoch, -(1:2)] <- theta
  }
  return(results)
}
@

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "gradient_descent")|
\newpage

Once implemented, the same function produces the three histories for Experiment B:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
full_run <- gradient_descent(y, X, batch_size = nrow(X),
                             eta = 0.1, epochs = 500)
set.seed(4711)
single_run <- gradient_descent(y, X, batch_size = 1,
                               eta = 0.1, epochs = 500)
set.seed(4711)
mini_run <- gradient_descent(y, X, batch_size = 10,
                             eta = 0.1, epochs = 500)
@

\item (4p) \textbf{Experiment A: learning rates with full gradient descent.}
Set the batch size to $n$ and run the function for 500 epochs from $\theta=0$, keeping the learning rate fixed within each run. Choose three learning rates that illustrate slow progress, useful convergence, and unstable or oscillating behaviour. Only full GD is needed for this experiment.

Submit \emph{one composite figure}: use three columns for the learning-rate settings and two rows for the mean NLL and one coefficient of your choice. Record the actual learning rates in the panel labels. Add the corresponding estimates from \texttt{glm()} as reference lines: its mean NLL in the loss panels and its coefficient estimate in the coefficient panels. Separate vertical scales are allowed so an unstable run does not hide the other curves.

Briefly explain how the three learning rates affect the loss and coefficient trajectories. A persistently oscillating loss is sufficient to demonstrate instability; you do not need to force the loss to become infinite.

\item (4p) \textbf{Experiment B: batch sizes at a shared learning rate.}
Call the same \texttt{gradient\_descent()} function with batch sizes $n$, 10, and 1 to compare full GD, mini-batch GD, and SGD. Use the same data and 500 epochs for all three. Start with a shared learning rate of $\eta=0.1$. If any run is unstable, reduce the learning rate for \emph{all three} methods. Set the random seed before each stochastic run and report the common learning rate and seed.

Submit \emph{one composite figure with two panels}: overlay the three methods' mean-NLL curves in one panel and their trajectories for the same coefficient used in Experiment A in the other. Use a legend and the \texttt{glm()} reference lines.

Compare progress and fluctuations near the reference estimates. Report the number of parameter updates per epoch for each method. An epoch processes all $n$ observations, but full GD makes one update, mini-batch GD makes $\lceil n/10\rceil$ updates, and SGD makes $n$ updates. Explain why equal epochs do not mean equal numbers of updates; this experiment is not a wall-clock timing comparison.
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\subsubsection*{Plotting hint for the two experiments}
Reuse your saved mean-loss and coefficient histories; no additional optimisation runs are needed for plotting. To find the three learning-rate behaviours, try a short logarithmic sequence of learning rates, such as $0.001$, $0.01$, $0.1$, $1$, and $10$, then adjust only if needed.

Collect the three result matrices for one experiment in a named list called \texttt{runs}. For example, its names can describe the learning rates in Experiment A or the methods in Experiment B. Each matrix should have the columns \texttt{epoch}, \texttt{nll}, and the parameter names from \texttt{X}. The following code only reshapes and plots results; use the output of your own optimizers.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(ggplot2)
# Here full_run, mini_run, and single_run are your saved histories.
runs <- list("Full GD" = full_run, "Mini-batch GD" = mini_run,
             "SGD" = single_run)
coefficient <- "gre_sd" # Choose one coefficient for both experiments.
plot_data <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(runs), function(label){
  h <- runs[[label]]
  rbind(
    data.frame(epoch = h[, "epoch"], value = h[, "nll"],
               measure = "Mean NLL", setting = label),
    data.frame(epoch = h[, "epoch"], value = h[, coefficient],
               measure = "Coefficient", setting = label)
  )
}))
plot_data$measure <- factor(plot_data$measure,
                           levels = c("Mean NLL", "Coefficient"))
plot_data$setting <- factor(plot_data$setting, levels = names(runs))
@

Construct the two reference values from \texttt{glm\_fit} and plot Experiment B as follows:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
theta_mle <- unname(coef(glm_fit)) # Same parameter order as X.
reference <- data.frame(
  measure = factor(c("Mean NLL", "Coefficient"),
                   levels = c("Mean NLL", "Coefficient")),
  value = c(nll(y, X, theta_mle),
            theta_mle[match(coefficient, colnames(X))])
)
ggplot(plot_data, aes(epoch, value, colour = setting)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_hline(data = reference, aes(yintercept = value),
             linetype = "dashed") +
  facet_wrap(~ measure, ncol = 1, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(x = "Epoch", y = NULL, colour = "Setting")
@
For Experiment A, prepare \texttt{runs} with its three learning-rate results and replace the facet line with \texttt{facet\_wrap(\textasciitilde{} measure + setting, ncol = 3, scales = "free\_y")}. This produces the six panels in one figure. You can choose another plotting tool or layout that makes the same comparisons clear.

