\section{Regularized Regression}

The datasets \texttt{prob2\_train} and \texttt{prob2\_test} contain simulated data with 240 explanatory variables (\texttt{V1-V240}) and 1 numerical response variable (\texttt{y}). As per the dataset names, the first dataset contains training data and the second contains test data for this problem. To access the data, just run:

<<eval=TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("prob2_train")
data("prob2_test")
dim(prob2_train)
X <- as.matrix(prob2_train[,-241])
y <- as.matrix(prob2_train[,"y"])
X_test <- as.matrix(prob2_test[,-241])
y_test <- as.matrix(prob2_test[,"y"])
@


\examplebox{Non-unique coefficients}{
With two observations and three coefficients, consider
\[
X=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\1&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\theta_A=(1,1,0)^\top,\quad \theta_B=(0,2,1)^\top.
\]
Both give $X\theta=(1,2)^\top$. For a new row $(1,0,0)$, they predict 1 and 0, respectively. A perfect training fit can coexist with non-unique coefficients; here $X^\top X$ is singular.
}

You should do the following and present the results in your report:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\thesection\alph*.}, ref=\thesection\alph*, leftmargin=*]
\item (1p) Fit a linear model to the training data. What are the results? Why does this happen?
\item (1p) Use \texttt{glmnet()} function from the \texttt{glmnet} package to fit a linear lasso regression to the training data with $\lambda=1$. How many predictor coefficients are nonzero, excluding the intercept? \emph{Hint!} \texttt{coefficients()}
\item Split the data into ten folds randomly.
\emph{Hint!} Use the \texttt{sample()} function and create a new variable called fold. Here is an example on how to select the folds
<<eval=FALSE>>=
fold <- sample(rep(1:10, length.out = nrow(X)))

X_trainfold1 <- X[fold!=1,]
X_valfold1 <- X[fold==1,]
y_trainfold1 <- y[fold!=1,]
y_valfold1 <- y[fold==1,]
@
\newpage

\item (5p) Implement 10-fold cross-validation on the training data. Include the function code once and use the $\lambda=1$ result requested below to demonstrate it.
\emph{Hint!} Implement this as \texttt{glmnet\_cv(fold, X, y, lambda)}. Use the supplied ten folds and return one number: the mean of the ten validation-fold RMSEs. Accept either a response vector or a one-column response matrix. Use lasso (\texttt{alpha = 1}) with the default Gaussian family, intercept, and standardisation in \texttt{glmnet()}. Keep the same folds when comparing different values of \texttt{lambda}.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\theenumi.\roman*., ref=\theenumi.\roman*, leftmargin=*]
\item For each fold, hold out the validation fold and train on the rest of the training set. See below.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
mod <- glmnet(X_trainfold, y_trainfold,
              alpha = 1, lambda = lambda)
pred_y_valfold <- predict(mod, newx = X_valfold)
@
\item Use the \texttt{rmse()} function in the \texttt{uuml} R package to compute the root mean squared error (RMSE) for each fold as follows:
<<eval=FALSE>>=
rmse(pred_y_valfold, y_valfold)
@
\item Take the mean RMSE for the 10 folds.
\end{enumerate}

\emph{Check:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "glmnet_cv")|
\item (1p) Now, compute the RMSE for $\lambda=1$ using 10-fold cross-validation.
\item (2p) Test different values for the hyper parameter $\lambda$. What is the best RMSE you can get with 10-fold cross-validation? What is the $\lambda$ for this value?
\item (1p) Refit the model with the selected $\lambda$ on all training observations, then report its RMSE on the test set. Use the test set only for this final evaluation.
\end{enumerate}

