\section{* Gradient Descent for penalized logistic regression}
\label{sec:ridge}

For the optional distinction task, complete either this section or Section~\ref{sec:adam}.

Reuse the binary-admission data from Section~\ref{sec:gd}.

<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
data("binary")

binary$gre_sd <- (binary$gre - mean(binary$gre))/sd(binary$gre)
binary$gpa_sd <- (binary$gpa - mean(binary$gpa))/sd(binary$gpa)
X <- model.matrix(admit ~ gre_sd + gpa_sd, binary)
y <- binary$admit
@

As in Section~\ref{sec:gd}, we minimize the mean negative log-likelihood. With a ridge penalty, the loss is
\begin{align}
\text{NLL}_r(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}, \lambda) = \text{NLL}(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X}) + \frac{\lambda}{2} \sum_{j=2}^P \theta_j^2\,,
\end{align}
where $\text{NLL}$ is the mean negative log-likelihood defined in Section~\ref{sec:gd} and $\lambda \geq 0$. The first column of $\mathbf{X}$ is the intercept column, so $\theta_1$ is excluded from the penalty \citep[see][Ch. 3.4]{hastie2009elements}.

\examplebox{The unpenalized intercept}{
For $\theta=(2,0.4,-0.3)^\top$ (intercept first) and $\lambda=1$, the ridge penalty is
\[
\frac12\bigl(0.4^2+(-0.3)^2\bigr)=0.125.
\]
Changing the intercept from 2 to 20 leaves this penalty unchanged, although the likelihood loss can change.
}

This scaling matches the logistic ridge objective in \texttt{glmnet}; changing the likelihood scaling changes the meaning of $\lambda$. More information can be found \href{https://glmnet.stanford.edu/articles/glmnet.html#logistic-regression-family-binomial-}{here}.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\thesection\alph*.}, ref=\thesection\alph*, leftmargin=*]
\item (2p) Derive the gradient for $\text{NLL}_r(\theta, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{X},\lambda)$ with respect to $\theta$.
\item (1p) Implement the gradient as \texttt{nllr\_grad(y, X, theta, lambda)}. It should return the gradient of $\text{NLL}_r$, including the penalty, with the intercept unpenalized. Include the function code and your actual outputs for the three calls below; compare them with the supplied results to four decimal places.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 0)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
c("(Intercept)" = 0.1825, "gre_sd" = -0.0857, "gpa_sd" = -0.0829)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(0,0,0), lambda = 1)
@

<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad <- function(y, X, theta, lambda){
    c("(Intercept)" = -0.0217, "gre_sd" = 0.5395, "gpa_sd" = 0.5426)
}
@
<<echo=TRUE,eval=TRUE>>=
nllr_grad(y, X, theta = c(-1,0.5,0.5), lambda = 1)
@

\emph{Select the ridge checks and check your gradient:}
<<mma-nllr-grad,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
set_assignment(paste("https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/",
                     "blob/master/assignments/tests/assignment1/",
                     "assignment1-vg-ridge.yml", sep = ""))
mark_my_assignment(tasks = "nllr_grad")
@
\item (1p) Implement the penalized loss $\text{NLL}_r$ as \texttt{nllr(y, X, theta, lambda)}. Include the function code and your actual outputs for the three calls below; compare them with the supplied results to four decimal places.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
