\emph{With the ridge checks selected:} \verb|mark_my_assignment(tasks = "nllr")|
\item (1p) Use \texttt{glmnet} to estimate \texttt{theta} for logistic ridge regression. Set \texttt{family = "binomial"}, \texttt{lambda = 1}, \texttt{alpha = 0}, and \texttt{standardize = FALSE} so the fit uses the same loss as your implementation on the already standardized predictors. Remove the intercept column from \texttt{X} when calling \texttt{glmnet}, and retain its default \texttt{intercept = TRUE} so it estimates an unpenalized intercept.
\item (2p) Implement \texttt{gradient\_descent\_reg()} by adapting your \texttt{gradient\_descent()} function. Reuse its batching and update logic, but use \texttt{nllr\_grad()} for the gradient and \texttt{nllr()} for the loss, passing \texttt{lambda} to both. Start from $\theta=0$. Return an \texttt{epochs} by $\texttt{ncol(X)}+2$ matrix with columns \texttt{epoch}, \texttt{nllr}, and the parameter names from \texttt{X}. Each row records the penalized loss on all training observations and the parameters after one complete epoch.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
