\section{* Implementation of the Adam optimizer}
\label{sec:adam}

For the optional distinction task, complete either this section or Section~\ref{sec:ridge}.

Reuse the binary-admission data from Section~\ref{sec:gd}.

<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
data("binary")

binary$gre_sd <- (binary$gre - mean(binary$gre))/sd(binary$gre)
binary$gpa_sd <- (binary$gpa - mean(binary$gpa))/sd(binary$gpa)
X <- model.matrix(admit ~ gre_sd + gpa_sd, binary)
y <- binary$admit
@
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{\thesection\alph*.}, ref=\thesection\alph*, leftmargin=*]

\item (2p) Implement the Adam mini-batch gradient descent algorithm \citep[see][for details and psuedo-code]{ruder2016overview} and \citep[][ for details]{kingma2014adam} as an R function. Not that in \citet{kingma2014adam}, the stepsize is denoted by $\alpha$.

\emph{Note!} Each epoch should include all data points. A tip is to order the data points randomly in each iteration.

Use the numerical constant $\epsilon=10^{-8}$ in the denominator. Retain the first and second moments across epochs, and advance the bias-correction step once per mini-batch update.

\examplebox{Which counter goes in the bias correction?}{
Adam corrects for zero initialization of the moments using
\[
\hat m_t=\frac{m_t}{1-\beta_1^t},\qquad
\hat v_t=\frac{v_t}{1-\beta_2^t}.
\]
Here $t$ counts mini-batch updates of the whole parameter vector. With 400 observations and batch size 10, epoch 1 uses $t=1,\ldots,40$; the first update of epoch 2 uses $t=41$, not 2. Carry the counter and moments across epochs; initialize them to zero only for a new run.
}

\newpage

A boilerplate for the algorithm can be found here.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
adam <- function(y, X, sample_size, eta, beta1, beta2, epochs){
  # Setup output matrix
  results <- matrix(0.0, ncol = ncol(X) + 2L, nrow = epochs)
  colnames(results) <- c("epoch", "nll", colnames(X))

  # Run algorithm
  theta <- rep(0.0, ncol(X)) # Init theta to the 0 vector
  for(j in 1:epochs){

    ### Put the algorithm code here ###

    # Store epoch, mean NLL and parameter values
    results[j, "epoch"] <- j
    results[j, "nll"] <- nll(y, X, theta)
    results[j, -(1:2)] <- theta
  }
  return(results)
}
@

\emph{Select the Adam checks and check your function:}
<<mma-adam,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
set_assignment(paste("https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/",
                     "blob/master/assignments/tests/assignment1/",
                     "assignment1-vg-adam.yml", sep = ""))
mark_my_assignment(tasks = "adam")
@
For example, run the baseline as follows:
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
baseline <- adam(y, X, sample_size = 10, eta = 0.01,
                 beta1 = 0.9, beta2 = 0.999, epochs = 500)
@
\item Print the function once in your report.
\item Run these three settings using fresh calls with batch size 10 and 500 epochs. Set the seed to 4711 before each call.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
Setting & $\eta$ & $\beta_1$ & $\beta_2$ \\
\hline
Baseline & 0.01 & 0.9 & 0.999 \\
Larger learning rate & 0.1 & 0.9 & 0.999 \\
Longer gradient memory & 0.01 & 0.99 & 0.999 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Submit \emph{one figure with two panels}, overlaying the three runs and adding the corresponding \texttt{glm()} reference lines. Reuse the plotting hint above to show:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\theenumi.\roman*., ref=\theenumi.\roman*, leftmargin=*]
\item (1p) Full-training-data mean NLL against epoch.
\item (1p) One coefficient against epoch.
\end{enumerate}
Briefly compare each modified run with the baseline: how do the learning rate and gradient memory affect progress and fluctuations? Small observed differences are valid.
\end{enumerate}

